WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.200
- I've told you in the past

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.794
that I take your comments seriously.

00:00:02.794 --> 00:00:05.390
Well now I'm putting my
money where my mouth is

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:08.080
and watching an episode
that you asked me to watch.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.611
Here we go, House M.D.,
episode "Three Stories."

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:14.194
(upbeat music)

00:00:17.047 --> 00:00:17.880
(static bristles)

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.463
(upbeat music)

00:00:26.242 --> 00:00:27.192
(static bristles)

00:00:27.192 --> 00:00:29.463
- [House] Three guys walk into a clinic.

00:00:29.463 --> 00:00:31.119
Their legs hurt.

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:32.560
(film squeaking)

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.981
In less than two hours, one of these three

00:00:34.981 --> 00:00:37.130
will be tossed out of the hospital

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:39.440
because they were faking
it to score narcotics

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.319
and one will be very close to death.

00:00:42.319 --> 00:00:43.152
(film squeaking)

00:00:43.152 --> 00:00:44.340
Where is the pain localized?

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.381
- It started just above my
ankle and it's radiating up.

00:00:47.381 --> 00:00:49.909
- So, what should we do first?

00:00:49.909 --> 00:00:51.350
- Family history?

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:53.080
- Indicative of leg pain?

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:54.190
That's a very short list.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:57.390
Any history of bone cancer,
osteogenesis imperfecta,

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.370
or multiple myeloma?

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.920
- It could be a blood issue.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.460
We should run a CBC and a d-dimer.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:04.710
- And get an MRI.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:05.960
- MRI or a PET scan?

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.550
- They're asking him
questions really out of order.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:12.966
In medical school you're taught
to go with the OPQRST model.

00:01:12.966 --> 00:01:16.794
O being onset, P being
provocative palliative

00:01:16.794 --> 00:01:19.130
Q being the quality of the pain,

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:22.240
R radiation of the pain, S
as the severity of the pain

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.200
and T timing which could
mean like time of day

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.700
or if they've ever had
this issue in the past.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.420
You don't just randomly start asking

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.180
family history out of nowhere.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.710
Because if you start asking that,

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:34.291
it could lead you down
a line of questioning

00:01:34.291 --> 00:01:36.100
where you're gonna spend a lot of time

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.660
and not get any valuable info.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.775
You want the high-yield stuff up front.

00:01:39.775 --> 00:01:42.125
- Sorry, thanks for playing.

00:01:42.125 --> 00:01:43.381
Patient's dead.

00:01:43.381 --> 00:01:44.903
You killed him.

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:48.340
- We had no time to run any tests,

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:49.173
there's nothing we could do.

00:01:49.173 --> 00:01:51.032
- You had time to look at the leg.

00:01:51.032 --> 00:01:54.410
(film squeaking)

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:55.470
Puncture.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:56.460
- [Female doctor] Snakebite.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:57.480
- That would be my guess.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.870
- So a lot of times when
you have an open wound

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:01.256
like this farmer's presenting with,

00:02:01.256 --> 00:02:04.290
you have to think,
obviously, about bug bites,

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:05.930
animal bites that sort of thing,

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.210
because we're in the northeast coast,

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.350
we always jump to Lyme disease, ticks.

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:11.377
*patient grunting*

00:02:11.377 --> 00:02:13.280
- Too much pain to be a slipped disc,

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.220
could be herniated and
appending the nerve root.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.670
- Or it could be referred
pain from his groin.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.738
- Sir, are you getting pain anywhere?

00:02:20.738 --> 00:02:22.390
- He's curling, it's not the back.

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.296
- Sir, where are you getting pain?

00:02:24.296 --> 00:02:25.653
- Help me!

00:02:25.653 --> 00:02:26.640
- Hey, he's not gonna tell us anything

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.280
if we don't get him out of pain.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:29.860
Give him 50 milligrams of Demerol.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:30.700
- We have no history.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:31.860
He could be allergic.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:32.850
- What do I do?

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.487
- We can't diagnose him while he screams.

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:36.873
- Better than killing him
with painkillers that-

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:40.460
- There's ways you can distinguish whether

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:41.950
someone's actually in pain or not.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:43.450
If someone's in horrendous pain,

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:44.490
their heart rate's gonna go up,

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:45.740
you can't fake that.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:47.670
You can't make your heart rate go up

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.150
while you're faking pain.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:50.790
Second of all, you can, when you're doing

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.840
your physical exam,
there's ways to test if

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.200
someone's really having pain.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:55.800
I do this all the time in the hospital.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.920
I'll put pressure on the area while the

00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.950
patient is looking at me
and I'll ask if it hurts.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:01.499
If they're screaming in pain,

00:03:01.499 --> 00:03:03.610
I'll change the subject or I'll ask them

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:04.780
about something else that's going on,

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.940
but leave my hand on the area where

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:08.150
they're still having pain.

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:09.120
And while I'm talking to them,

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.490
I'll gently start increasing
pressure on that area.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:12.680
If they're not jumping in pain,

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:14.769
it's either not as bad as they say it is,

00:03:14.769 --> 00:03:16.880
or they may be pain seeking,

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:18.410
exhibiting pain seeking behavior.

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:21.080
This example doesn't look
like a person's faking pain.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:22.970
- You did exactly what his attending did.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:24.230
- [Male Doctor] And
that was the proper way

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:25.400
to handle the case?

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:26.233
- Yeah.

00:03:26.233 --> 00:03:27.660
- The guy used him as a dealer.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.735
- You're gonna see a lot
of drug seeking behavior

00:03:29.735 --> 00:03:32.753
in your practice, and
there's a reason- it works.

00:03:32.753 --> 00:03:33.586
(film squeaking)

00:03:33.586 --> 00:03:34.419
- [Dr. Cameron] This will start making you

00:03:34.419 --> 00:03:35.938
feel better really fast.

00:03:35.938 --> 00:03:38.855
(monitors beeping)

00:03:43.566 --> 00:03:44.760
He's having an allergic reaction.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:45.593
Bag.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:50.540
Paddles and epi.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:51.373
- His heart's fine.

00:03:51.373 --> 00:03:52.270
- It's not gonna stay that way.

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:54.530
- The reason you give
the epi is not because

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:55.690
his heart's about to about to stop,

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:58.460
it's because his airway's closing

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:00.160
from having an anaphylactic attack

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.300
to what looks like the snake anti-venom.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.710
So you need to give
epinephrine immediately,

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:07.160
usually to the upper thigh
or some other muscular region

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:08.890
where it can get well
absorbed very quickly.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:11.110
- Let's say we take five.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:12.980
Get some coffee, go pee.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:15.870
- It's funny, like he's fine
with the medical students

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.268
going out to do that.

00:04:17.268 --> 00:04:20.340
Nowadays, all the medical
students would be buried

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:23.130
in their phones, trying to
Google what the answer is

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:24.850
or Google what the possibilities are.

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:27.440
What a good doctor means
in medicine has changed

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.590
from those who know the most

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:32.244
to those who can get the best information

00:04:32.244 --> 00:04:34.560
in the most efficient way possible.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.060
Because there's so much information.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.710
It's being updated so quickly.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.750
It's almost impossible to be up-to-date

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.560
on every single study
on every single subject.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.370
I've heard that there was five studies

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:47.040
coming out per minute
in the scientific field,

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.930
so it's impossible to stay up-to-date with

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:50.290
every piece of literature,

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:53.020
but if you're very good
with internet research,

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:55.250
you're very good with verifying sources,

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:57.140
you're very good at
reading journal articles

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.732
and understanding which one's
the quality one versus not,

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:02.290
you can make a good decision and possibly

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:03.820
the best decision for your patient

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:05.213
from being able to research.

00:05:05.213 --> 00:05:06.546
- [Patient] Oof.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:09.210
- [Dr. Foreman] The patient
responded to epinephrine

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.186
and the allergic reaction was arrested.

00:05:11.186 --> 00:05:14.550
Unfortunately, the patient
continues to deteriorate.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:15.540
- [Dr. Chase] Maybe the snake wrangler

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:17.050
was wrong about the type he caught.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.430
- He faxed us the venom tests

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:20.430
and confirmed it's a timber rattlesnake.

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:23.950
- No, it's not.

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:25.260
Notice the volume?

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:27.310
- I skimmed over that and the gender

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:29.337
and the coloring, jumped right
to the name of the snake.

00:05:29.337 --> 00:05:30.170
*Dr. Mike laughs*

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:31.346
- 200 milligrams.

00:05:31.346 --> 00:05:34.400
Our guy got bit less than four hours ago.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:35.750
There's no way a snake regenerates

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:37.700
that much venom that quickly.

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:39.980
- We're supposed to know how
fast makes make the venom?

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:42.770
- Nope, unless you get
a patient bit by one.

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:44.350
- Okay, that was just ridiculous.

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:46.640
I mean, I get that
House has to know a lot,

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.910
but he doesn't know how fast
snakes regenerate their venom.

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:50.097
That's just insane.

00:05:50.097 --> 00:05:53.120
I don't know, I guess now I know that too,

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:55.362
but where does he get that?

00:05:55.362 --> 00:05:58.910
Snake's venom, regeneration?

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:00.424
- It hurts again.

00:06:00.424 --> 00:06:02.779
- [Female Doctor] He came back?

00:06:02.779 --> 00:06:05.618
- On average, drug addicts are stupid.

00:06:05.618 --> 00:06:06.451
(film squeaking)

00:06:06.451 --> 00:06:08.560
- We're gonna put this hard rubber tube up

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:10.880
your urethra and into your bladder.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:11.980
It may be a little uncomfortable.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:14.873
- Will I be getting
some sort of anesthetic?

00:06:14.873 --> 00:06:17.433
- We're concerned about
allergic reactions today.

00:06:17.433 --> 00:06:18.266
*groaning*

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:28.050
- If the guy can handle a rod in his penis

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.050
for a half an hour, he's really sick.

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:33.050
- Okay, you don't just put a catheter in

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:35.800
because you want to
see how serious someone

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:39.010
is about getting medications.

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:42.120
There are ways to check for
people who are malingering.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:43.745
Malingering is basically when a person

00:06:43.745 --> 00:06:47.380
is trying to act sick in order to get some

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:49.848
sort of secondary gain like drugs,

00:06:49.848 --> 00:06:52.420
like a day off work, like disability.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.950
These things do happen and
you need to consider them.

00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:56.880
Not first and foremost in your mind,

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:58.900
but they do need to be in your thought

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:00.290
process, in your differential.

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:01.770
- Wrong snake?

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:03.379
- Tried every other antivenom we had.

00:07:03.379 --> 00:07:04.940
- Or too late?

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:06.363
- Yep. He's dying.

00:07:06.363 --> 00:07:07.196
(film squeaking)

00:07:07.196 --> 00:07:08.029
- You're dying.

00:07:08.029 --> 00:07:08.987
*Dr. Mike laughs*

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:11.403
In a few hours.

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:16.690
There's nothing we can do
except deal with the pain.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:20.500
- Well, I need to go home.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:21.700
