00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:16.010
- Compartment syndrome?

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:21.010
- It was an aneurysm that
clotted, leading to an infarction.

00:10:22.397 --> 00:10:23.703
- God, you were right.

00:10:23.703 --> 00:10:24.543
It's House.

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:28.230
- [Female Doctor] We
have to do the surgery.

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:30.630
- Now I get why you guys
recommended this episode.

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:32.196
I was wondering what happened to House,

00:10:32.196 --> 00:10:34.310
unless this is a whole thing is a farce.

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:36.490
It would probably take a
few days to diagnose it

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:40.292
because it's such a
unique and rare condition,

00:10:40.292 --> 00:10:44.050
that it wouldn't appear
at the top of your list.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:46.340
If you're thinking of aneurysm
at the top of your list,

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:48.840
you're gonna be doing a
lot of unnecessary MRIs

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:52.140
and missing a lot of problems
that are much more common.

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:54.770
So you need to think horses, not zebras.

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:56.530
But I know since this House M.D.,

00:10:56.530 --> 00:10:59.030
zebras are usually the
first thing they talk about.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:00.230
- Do a bypass.

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:01.530
Restore the circulation.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:03.090
- Amputation is safer.

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:04.390
- For you or me?

00:11:04.390 --> 00:11:05.690
- The blockage of blood flow-

00:11:05.690 --> 00:11:07.124
- Four day blockage.

00:11:07.124 --> 00:11:08.800
- Yes.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.210
It caused muscle cell death.

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:13.300
When those cells die, they
release cytokines and potassium.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:15.530
- To restore the blood flow
instead of just lopping

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:17.440
it all off then all that
crap gets washed back

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:20.450
into my system and cytokines
can cause organ failure

00:11:20.450 --> 00:11:22.270
and the potassium could
cause cardiac arrest.

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:25.060
On the other hand, can we just
get the use of my leg back?

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:26.140
- This is really interesting.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:28.103
So, whenever you have a part of the body

00:11:28.103 --> 00:11:30.100
that doesn't have blood going through it

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:31.150
for a very long period of time,

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:33.620
that body, the part of the body, dies.

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:36.030
Becomes necrotic, that's
medical term for it.

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:38.810
Because of ischemia,
lack of oxygen, it dies,

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:41.230
because blood is bringing
oxygen to those areas.

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:43.380
When a muscle dies, for example,

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:46.310
it releases these inflammatory
cells called cytokines.

00:11:46.310 --> 00:11:49.610
It also releases potassium
because when a cell ruptures,

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:51.380
the first thing that releases or the most

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:54.040
important thing that it
releases, is potassium.

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:55.850
And having too high of a potassium

00:11:55.850 --> 00:11:57.760
in your bloodstream is very dangerous.

00:11:57.760 --> 00:11:58.593
It can be lethal.

00:11:58.593 --> 00:12:00.930
And having too much of inflammatory cells

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:03.230
can also be lethal and cause organ damage.

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:04.690
So you have that problem going on

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:06.880
because his muscles
have been without blood

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:08.500
for a long period of time in his leg,

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:11.010
but then as you restore blood flow

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:13.270
you have reperfusion of that area.

00:12:13.270 --> 00:12:16.540
You can also get even
more inflammation going

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:18.600
and inflammatory markers going,

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:22.010
because he's bringing healthy
blood into damaged blood,

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:24.490
and that sparks to even more inflammation.

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:25.780
And that's why Dr. Cuddy's actually

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:27.710
encouraging him to go for the amputation,

00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:29.550
to leave all those cytokines and

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:32.330
inflammatory cells and
potassium in the leg

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:34.970
and not cause even more
inflammation and pain.

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:36.490
But I also understand
what he's coming from,

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:38.100
where he doesn't want to lose his leg.

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:40.440
It's risky what he's saying.

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:42.680
It's not advisable, what he's saying.

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:44.100
And I could see a lot of surgeons

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:45.848
turning down this procedure because

00:12:45.848 --> 00:12:49.730
they don't see that this is ideal for him.

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:51.010
As a doctor, you always want to do

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:52.427
what's best for the patient and

00:12:52.427 --> 00:12:55.540
let the patient decide
what's best for their bodies,

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:57.620
but if they're telling
you to do a procedure

00:12:57.620 --> 00:13:00.230
that you think is very
risky and unnecessary,

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:01.800
you're well within your means to try

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:02.890
and convince them otherwise.

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:05.040
- If you were right, the
pain would be subsiding.

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:06.434
You'd be getting better.

00:13:06.434 --> 00:13:08.220
- It's just taking longer.

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:09.083
- No, it's not.

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:13.433
We've got to let them cut the leg off.

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:17.043
- It's my leg.

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:19.884
It's my life.

00:13:19.884 --> 00:13:20.717
(film squeaking)

00:13:20.717 --> 00:13:23.077
- The middle ground you
were talking about -

00:13:23.077 --> 00:13:25.003
- We go in, take out the dead muscle.

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:28.303
There's still some risk of
reperfusion injury, but-

00:13:28.303 --> 00:13:30.497
- Give me the forms you need signed.

00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:36.700
- If I were House, I'd
be pretty mad about that.

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:39.980
But then again, this is
why the bioethics committee

00:13:39.980 --> 00:13:42.500
comes into play because you're technically

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:44.370
doing something the patient doesn't want,

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:46.460
it is explicitly said he doesn't want,

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:48.810
but you know it's better for them.

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:49.820
It's such a thin line.

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:52.280
I'm not sure what the right call here is.

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:54.870
But as medical proxy,
she does have say over

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:56.620
what happens to his body right now.

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:02.563
- Because of the extent
the muscle removed,

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:07.690
utility of the patient's leg
was severely compromised.

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:11.140
Because of the time delay
in making the diagnosis,

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:13.970
the patient continues to
experience chronic pain.

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:15.310
- A lot of people don't realize this,

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:17.940
but when you're on chronic
opioid medications,

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:21.700
which is just essentially it's
a masking agent for the pain,

00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:24.240
it's not treating your
pain, just hiding the pain.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:25.820
When you're on that for
a long period of time,

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:27.600
it changes the chemistry of your brain,

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:30.740
and actually makes you feel
something known as hyperalgesia,

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:35.070
where you feel more pain and
are more sensitive to pain,

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:38.470
so that sometimes in a
patient who has chronic pain,

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:41.750
you have to reduce the amount
of pain medications they take

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:44.670
for them over time to feel less pain.

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:47.500
It's a paradoxical, complicated concept,

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:48.727
but I've actually had a patient that,

00:14:48.727 --> 00:14:51.610
he was 81 years old,
30 years of back pain,

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:54.380
he's been on opiate
medications for two years,

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:57.877
he's taken morphine and
when he came to me said,

00:14:57.877 --> 00:15:00.040
"Dr. Varshavski, you're my last hope".

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:01.760
And when I talked to him about it,

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:03.039
I said that I know this
is gonna sound crazy,

00:15:03.039 --> 00:15:06.340
but I have to lower your dose

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:09.760
and eventually get you off
of your narcotic medications

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:12.080
if you want to improve this pain.

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:14.248
And actually, right now we're at a point

00:15:14.248 --> 00:15:18.780
where he's not taking any
narcotic medications for his pain.

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:20.990
He's going to the gym, he's golfing,

00:15:20.990 --> 00:15:22.470
he's felt better than ever.

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:24.580
Narcotics aren't always the right answer.

00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:27.020
They have a place, there's
definitely a role for them,

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:28.840
but they're not always the right answer.

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:31.070
They definitely have harms and sometimes

00:15:31.070 --> 00:15:32.300
those harms outweigh the benefits

00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:33.852
and we need not lie about that.

00:15:33.852 --> 00:15:35.403
- [Female Doctor] She
had no right to do that.

00:15:35.403 --> 00:15:36.730
- She had the proxy.

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:38.090
- She knew he didn't want the surgery.

00:15:38.090 --> 00:15:39.222
- She saved his life!

00:15:39.222 --> 00:15:41.120
- We don't know that, maybe
he would have been fine.

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:43.080
- It doesn't matter,
it's the patients call.

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:44.107
- The patient's an idiot.

00:15:44.107 --> 00:15:45.600
*Dr. Mike laughs*

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:46.433
- Little do they know!

00:15:46.433 --> 00:15:47.552
- They usually are.

00:15:47.552 --> 00:15:50.302
(film squeaking)

00:15:53.246 --> 00:15:54.079
(film squeaking)

00:15:54.079 --> 00:15:55.570
- It wasn't clear to me in the episode

00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:57.040
if they understand that it's him,

00:15:57.040 --> 00:15:58.550
because they were still talking smack

00:15:58.550 --> 00:16:00.770
at the end of that episode.

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:03.090
I do think that sometimes
he crosses the line

00:16:03.090 --> 00:16:05.440
and is a little rude,
but that's Dr. House.

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:08.350
He's just got this
personality that's like angry

00:16:08.350 --> 00:16:11.270
and wants everyone to
see him as the bad guy,

00:16:11.270 --> 00:16:14.330
when in reality, he wants
to do the right thing

00:16:14.330 --> 00:16:16.020
and he wants to help people,

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:17.760
but he comes off harsh at times.

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:20.198
I feel like whenever he
says a patient is dumb

00:16:20.198 --> 00:16:22.220
or doesn't do the right thing,

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:23.890
he's essentially thinking that because

00:16:23.890 --> 00:16:26.200
he's thinking about the mistake he made

00:16:26.200 --> 00:16:29.298
of not amputating his leg
and going through all those

00:16:29.298 --> 00:16:34.270
surgeries and procedures and
having this chronic pain issue,

00:16:34.270 --> 00:16:36.730
when if he just would
have gotten an amputation,

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:40.250
got a replacement, you
know, there's so many ways

00:16:40.250 --> 00:16:42.000
to get a prosthetic nowadays.

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.530
If you have another episode
in the later seasons,

00:16:44.530 --> 00:16:46.390
season two maybe season three,

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:48.090
drop it down below in the comments.

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:49.680
I'll watch that episode.

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:51.337
I want to watch more of this series.

00:16:51.337 --> 00:16:53.270
I like watching House.

00:16:53.270 --> 00:16:55.330
It refreshes a lot of my knowledge

00:16:55.330 --> 00:16:57.340
that I know I studied back in the day,

00:16:57.340 --> 00:16:58.940
but I haven't really utilized.

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:00.470
This is a great way for me to learn

00:17:00.470 --> 00:17:02.168
and for you guys to learn at home.

00:17:02.168 --> 00:17:04.630
So keep those comments coming.

00:17:04.630 --> 00:17:06.780
Subscribe if you like my content,

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:08.510
